---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: device-runtime
covers: subtree
purpose: Device-private executable custody and ephemeral session identity, independent of transport.
rules:
  - id: rust-style-strict
    policy: rust-style-strict@1
---

The release record and session pins are private device records (B); the status view is
reconstructed (C). No new DHT entries, coordinator calls, signals or recurring network heads.
Binary identity uses eprfs-core raw CIDs; release manifests use its canonical DAG-CBOR codec.
Ingest verifies bytes; land alone advances current after provenance, ancestry and health.
Health notes a candidate whose `peer serve --check` is unsupported or finds no device, but
refuses both on a forward landing once the caller passes `PeerServing::Required` (epr-cli
does when the device manifest head declares a supervised `peer serve`; an unreadable head
keeps the note, warned). A revert still lands with the note: it is the escape hatch.
The launcher trusts ingested immutable files, as the preceding Python exec path did.
Private local custody has no public reach claim. Cross-device custody belongs to slice B.

Adopting a deployment base carries the device's own process declarations forward: every
head process the head's own base did not declare identically. So a fleet base cannot
force-remove or replace a process the device declared or edited; a base naming one
differently refuses until `--replace-processes`, and storage boot keeps the previous head
with a warning instead of failing. Refusing by default would wedge every act once the base
moved, and its cure (replace, then re-declare) commits a head without the resident in
between, which the supervisor would stop and restart. Base-owned processes still follow
the base, so a deployment removes those as before.
